(7. Data Quality Considerations) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
48. 527.8899999999999864 Td
(- **Proxy reporting**: In PLFS, household head or one informed member may report for all household members,) Tj
T* (causing classification errors for women and youth.) Tj
T* (- **Misclassification of contributing family workers**: Workers in family enterprises may be reported as homemakers if) Tj
T* (they don't perceive their work as "employment.") Tj
T* (- **Seasonal bias**: If fieldwork is concentrated in a particular season, annual estimates may not be representative of) Tj
T* (the full year.) Tj
T* (- **Response burden**: Quarterly follow-up for panel households can lead to respondent fatigue and attrition.) Tj
T* (- **Comparability over time**: Changes in survey design, questionnaire, or processing can break time series. Document) Tj
T* (methodological changes in metadata.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 13 Tf
14.9499999999999993 TL
0.059 0.09 0.165 rg
48. 390.8899999999999864 Td
(8. Key Learning Points) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
48. 372.8899999999999864 Td
(1. Employment-unemployment indicators are estimated from PLFS using weighted survey data with complex design) Tj
T* (adjustments.) Tj
T* (2. The three reference periods \(UPS, CWS, CDS\) produce different indicator values; CDS gives the broadest measure) Tj
T* (of underemployment.) Tj
T* (3. ILO/ICLS 2013 definitions require all three conditions \(without work, available, seeking\) for unemployment) Tj
T* (classification.) Tj
T* (4. Standard errors must be computed using methods appropriate for complex survey designs \(Taylor linearisation,) Tj
T* (jackknife, bootstrap\).) Tj
T* (5. Administrative data \(EPFO, ESIC, NPS\) supplement survey estimates for high-frequency formal employment) Tj
T* (monitoring.) Tj
T* (6. Low unemployment rates in India often reflect disguised underemployment rather than full employment.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 13 Tf
14.9499999999999993 TL
0.059 0.09 0.165 rg
48. 209.8899999999999864 Td
(9. Learning Outcomes) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
48. 191.8899999999999864 Td
(On completing this material, you should be able to:) Tj
T* (- Apply ILO/ICLS 2013 definitions to classify persons into employment status categories.) Tj
T* (- Explain the step-by-step methodology for estimating LFPR, WPR, and unemployment rate from PLFS data.) Tj
T* (- Compute employment indicators from survey data using appropriate weights.) Tj
T* (- Identify and account for complex survey design in standard error estimation.) Tj
T* (- Describe administrative data sources used to validate and supplement PLFS-based estimates.) Tj
T* (- Avoid common pitfalls in interpreting India's employment-unemployment statistics.) Tj
ET
BT
